ITO-coated glass substrates were ultrasonically cleaned in acetone for 15 minutes, followed by isopropyl alcohol for 15 minutes, then thermally annealed in a vacuum oven at $60 degree "C"$ for 20 minutes. The phosphate buffer electrolyte was prepared by mixing $25"mL"$ of $0.1"M" "KH"_2 "PO"_4$ and $25"mL"$ of $0.1"M" "Na"_2 "HPO"_4 dot 12 "H"_2 "O"$, yielding a pH of approximately 7.1. The acidic CA proton-doping buffer was prepared from $25"mL"$ of $0.1"M" "KCl"$, adjusted to pH 3 by adding aliquots of $0.1"M" "HCl"$.

Following the procedure described by @varolElectropolymerizationPolydopamineElectrodeSupported2023, electropolymerization was performed using an Autolab PGSTAT302N electrochemical workstation in a standard three-electrode cell configuration consisting of a working electrode (WE), a graphene sheet ($2 times 4 "cm"$) counter electrode (CE), and a SCE (Hg/$"Hg"_2"Cl"_2$) as the reference electrode (RE). Prior to deposition, dissolved oxygen was removed by purging the phosphate buffer under dry $"N"_2$ for 15 minutes to suppress premature self-oxidation of dopamine monomers. Dopamine hydrochloride was then added at a concentration of approximately 5 mM (equivalent to 1 mg/mL), followed by a second 15-minute purge. The PDA film was deposited by CV starting from $0"V"$ and sweeping between $-0.5"V"$ and $+0.5"V"$ (vs. SCE) for 10 cycles at a scan rate of $20"mV/s"$. The detailed chemical transformation pathway from dopamine to the polydopamine film is shown in @fig_appendix_chemical_structure_pda. For preliminary electrochemical characterization, the working electrode was a GCE; for device fabrication, the film was deposited directly on the patterned ITO substrate.

Following electropolymerization, the substrates were rinsed twice in deionized water to remove unreacted dopamine monomers and electrolyte residues. CA proton-doping was then performed in the acidic 0.1 M $"KCl"+ 0.1 M "HCl"$ buffer (pH 3). The 0.1 M KCl supplements the Cl⁻ from 0.1 M HCl, reducing solution resistance and providing excess counter-anions to stabilize the cationic polymer matrix during proton-doping without further lowering pH, by applying a constant potential of $+0.25"V"$ (vs. SCE) for $60"s"$ to convert the PDA film to the proton-doped state (@fig_appendix_dopamine_quinone). After proton-doping, substrates were rinsed again twice in deionized water to prevent KCl crystallization during subsequent annealing, dried with $"N"_2$ flow, placed on a glass petri dish, and annealed in a vacuum oven at $-0.08"MPa"$ at $75 degree "C"$ overnight, removing water and inducing thermal cross-linking of the PDA film.

Top contacts were deposited via electron-beam evaporation using a Kurt J. Lesker PRO Line PVD 75 system to complete the Cu/C/PDA/ITO crosspoint structures with a $10"nm"$ C interlayer at $0.1-0.15 "Å/s"$ to protect the PDA film, followed by a $50"nm"$ Cu electrode through a shadow mask at $<= 2.4 times 10^(-5) "Torr"$. The deposition rate for Cu was held at $0.2 "Å/s"$ for the initial $3-4"nm"$ and increased to $0.5 "Å/s"$ for the remaining thickness, with the substrate holder rotating at $0.5 "rpm"$.

== 3/ Electrical characterization

DC electrical characterization of the fabricated memristor crossbars was conducted using a Keithley 2400 SourceMeter coupled with a manual probe station. Bipolar DC current-voltage (I-V) sweeps were applied starting from 0 V to $+V_"max"$, sweeping back through 0 V to $-V_"max"$, and returning to 0 V to evaluate the memristive switching behavior.

Pulsed endurance, STP and PPF measurements were performed at the Guangdong Technion-Israel Institute of Technology (GTIIT), China, using a Keysight B1500A Semiconductor Device Analyzer equipped with a Waveform Generator/Fast Measurement Unit (WGFMU) module. Pulsed endurance testing used a logarithmic batch protocol where the initial linear count was set to 9000 cycles, after which a step-log of 50 was applied, capturing the resistance state at these logarithmic points up to the final $10^6$ cycles target. Short-term synaptic plasticity was characterized under single-pulse and multi-pulse configurations. Paired-Pulse Facilitation (PPF) was evaluated over two consecutive pulses using the ratio $"PPF Index" = (A_2 / A_1) times 100\%$, with net facilitation expressed as $Delta "PPF" = [(A_2 - A_1) / A_1] times 100\%$. For long pulse-train stimulation, cumulative synaptic plasticity was quantified by the Synaptic Gain Index ($A_50 / A_1 times 100\%$). Transient active energy consumption was evaluated using time-resolved numerical trapezoidal integration $E = integral V(t) I(t) dif t = sum [(V_k I_k + V_(k-1) I_(k-1)) / 2] Delta t$ during the active pulse transition, excluding post-pulse zero-voltage ($V(t) = 0$) capacitive discharge.

== 4/ Structural and Chemical Characterization

XPS was conducted at GTIIT using a PHI Versa Probe III system (ULVAC-PHI) with a monochromated $"Al" K alpha$ source ($1486.6 "eV"$), collecting high-resolution C 1s, O 1s, and N 1s core-level spectra. Most photoemission peaks were fitted using a GL(30) lineshape (Gaussian-Lorentzian product formula with 30% Lorentzian contribution), while the $pi$-$pi^*$ shake-up satellite was fitted using an LA(1.4, 1.4, 100) asymmetric lineshape.

SERS measurements were collected using a Horiba LabRAM HR Evolution spectrometer equipped with a 785 nm near-infrared (NIR) laser. The 785 nm excitation was chosen to suppress autofluorescence intrinsic to eumelanin-like polymers. Measurements were performed using a 10% of the laser power at the sample surface. Each spectrum was acquired for 20 s with 3 accumulations over a spectral range of 800-2000 cm⁻¹. SERS enhancement was achieved by drop-casting 5-10 µL of citrate-stabilized gold nanoparticles (50-60 nm) onto the film and drying at 50-60 °C for 5 minutes prior to acquisition.

Surface morphology, roughness, and thickness of the electropolymerized PDA films were characterized using an Oxford Instruments MFP-3D Origin AFM operating in contact mode. Step-height profiles across the patterned film edges were obtained to determine the physical thickness of the active switching layer across different deposition parameters and proton-doped states.

UV-Vis absorption spectra were collected using a spectrometer at the Institute of Physics, VAST to identify the transmission properties of PDA between normal and proton-doped states.
